lower Mekong region. The public report leaves them nameless; another version handed over to U.S. authorities identified all involved. \u201cWildlife trafficking in Southeast Asia is in the hands of Chinese nationals by 90%,\u201d Crosta said. He added that they have strong business ties in the region, with investments in real estate and a mix of legal and illegal businesses that include restaurants and clubs as well as scam centers. They are \u201cvery, very prolific\u201d wildlife traffickers, Crosta said. His team found that the two they identified as the largest traffickers own at least nine shops in Cambodia. Investigators saw many endangered wildlife products sold openly in their shops as well as online, sometimes to customers in other countries. Other businesses include a factory that carves rhino horn and ivory and a brewery for making tiger bone wine \u2014 an expensive alcoholic beverage brewed with tiger bones that\u2019s often sold in as online, sometimes to customers in other countries. Other businesses include a factory that carves rhino horn and ivory and a brewery for making tiger bone wine \u2014 an expensive alcoholic beverage brewed with tiger bones that\u2019s often sold in ornate tiger-shaped bottles. Though it\u2019s been used as traditional Chinese medicine for millennia, it\u2019s now become a pricey prestige item. Serving or gifting tiger bone wine is a way to flaunt status or seal a deal. Investigators also identified middlemen and buyers, including restaurant owners, businessmen and traffickers. One individual said he raised lions, tigers and primates on his properties \u2014 and allegedly trafficked primates, birds and bear parts, according to the investigation. Another said he smuggled wildlife products and narcotics to the West. Yet another said his role was logistics, claiming he could help traffic anything because he had close connections to Cambodian customs officials as well as the country\u2019s political, military and business elit